00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.880 align:start position:0%
 
as a person is going into the final year  of the physics degree at the University  of Oxford I've done quite a lot of study  in my time and I believe that if you  want to study the most effective you  should follow five simple rules rule  number one realize that learning is  individual there's this stereotype of  studying taking place in complete  silence in a library and that somehow if  you're not studying like that then  you're doing it wrong but the thing to  realize is that there is no such thing  as the wrong way to study if you work in  a particular way and find that it works  for you then you're doing it right  education comes from the Latin Educare a  meaning to draw forth and that's exactly  what studying is all about it's not  about stuffing your head full of facts  it is about making the most of your  potential and in order to do that you  have to tailor your studying I  personally work at my best in a library  in silence with earplugs in but then  sometimes I don't sometimes I've worked  better in an environment like a coffee  shop or my college common room where  there's the sound of other people  buzzing around you if you don't know  what works best for you then experiment  try working in different environments  and try working with music without music  with friends on your own once you find a  way that works for you stick with it  think about where you work at your best  so bedroom coffee shop library wherever  but also when you work at your best  again personally I prefer to work on new  material in theory in the morning and  then practice in the afternoon but that  varies from subject to subject again  experiment and see what works best for  you  rule number 2 managing your time this is  absolutely crucial managing your time is  all about making the job of studying as  easy as possible for you so if you don't  do it you're only making life harder for  yourself than you need to  when I say managing your time I mean on  two different timescales both on a daily  basis but also on a longer time scale of  terms and months on a daily basis plan  what you're going to do each day every  evening I'll write a to-do list of what  I want to accomplish tomorrow so that  would be things like do this problem  sheet finish this section notes whatever  and in doing that I'm trying to time  table about eight hours worth of work  and that's a good amount to aim for  every day in the long run in the couple  of weeks leading up to exams you're  probably gonna want to allow for a  couple more hours per day planning your  time so your revisit material is  absolutely crucial what I do is keep a  diary of work and then at the end of  each day write down what it was that  I've done that day then turn ahead one  week and write down a reminder to review  what I just done and then the same thing  for one month's time by writing in your  day's work you keep track of what you've  done and it allows you to look at it  tomorrow and see what you need to review  and you can incorporate that and you  were to do this for the next day if  you're consistent with it it becomes a  very powerful tool another tool that  people find really useful is using a  whiteboard and I use this a lot in the  past to keep track of deadlines so  whenever I'll set a problem sheet an  essay or an assignment whatever I would  write in on the whiteboard when I was  given it what I had to do and when it  was due in then put that in a visible  place and make sure that you check it  every day so you can never forget what  it is you have to do you can also use it  to plan ahead the next day's work or do  rough working for a problem sheet on it  in the longer term don't start working  too late you can always start revising  material before you finished everything  in class leaving everything to the last  minute only adds extra stress and means  you're never going to be able to perform  your best in exams sit down with a  calendar or diary and work out how many  weeks you've gotten to your exams then  write a list of all the material we need  to cover by them then decide how you  want to structure your time do you want  to do all the particular subject in one  go one subject at a time or would you  prefer to mix it up doing different  subjects in one day or in one week  personally I prefer the latter  then you need to drop a rough timetable  splitting up the time that's available  to you between the material that you  need to cover make sure that you have a  rough idea of when you want to get  ticking modules done so you can gauge  your progress and know if you're falling  behind schedule also when do you want to  practice personally I find it most  beneficial to do theory behind something  first then a day or so later do some  practice then in the week or a couple of  weeks before the exams get my hands on  as many passed papers as possible and  drill exam technique now some people  prefer to revise purely  pass papers though some personal  experience I wouldn't recommend that I  think it's better to focus on the  material making sure you understand it  first then practicing laws  rule number three manage your notes as I  said before learning is as individual as  people are and so notes that two people  make any two people make will never be  the same but I believe that there are  some basic rules you try and stick to  when making your note the first of those  is to try and keep your notes consistent  this year I probably own a bit OTT on  this and I wrote all of my notes on  exactly the same type of paper with  exactly the same type of pen with the  same color scheme and subheading  structure throughout and yeah that's  pretty bit over the top but keeping your  notes consistent I find helps me  synthesize information that bit better  plus it looks professional another one  of these rules is to realize that  certain subjects lend themselves to  certain types of making notes so physics  for example which involves lots of  richly detailed concepts and absolutely  loads of mathematical sections I  personally found it best to produce  notes which read like a textbook so I  even had a page numbering system and a  contents page so I could use it as a  reference and those notes those final  notes represent the culmination of notes  from textbooks notes from lectures and  notes from tutorials  however for something like anthropology  or geography which involves lots of  concepts with absolutely loads of case  studies and references it might be more  useful to produce something like an a3  mind map which links information  spatially to a concept also remember  that there are different types of notes  those two examples I've already given  textbook notes and mind maps are  examples of visual learning the idea  being that you look at the information  on the page and you remember it but just  as validly you could produce notes which  appeal to other stars of learning so  learning by hearing auditory learning  and learning by doing  kinesthetic learning examples of these  might be making recordings or videos of  yourself explaining concepts and then  playing them back as a revision or the  classroom classic is to imagine yourself  as an electron and then  think about how changing the voltage or  resistance of the circuit might change  the way that you move and remember that  you can always mix up different styles  and notes you can always do some visual  notes and some kinesthetic notes if that  will help you broadly speaking though  try and keep your notes whatever they  may be as consistent as you can and also  as clean and uncluttered as you can my  notes have loads of white space on the  page and they're written as clearly as I  can manage any clutter on the page is  only making your brain work harder than  absolutely has to and give it a break  rule number four take good care of  yourself yes I am gonna make this point  yes I know it is really boring but it is  so important you're never going to be  able to perform at your best if you are  not at your best so yeah do all those  things that your parents and teachers  have been telling you to do for years  try and get a good amount of sleep each  night if you if you can improv out eight  hours if you don't then your brains only  going to be tired and you won't be able  to take in new information or remember  old information try to exercise two or  three times a week go swimming go  running play some football go to the gym  whatever as well as making you fitter  exercise releases and dolphins which are  the body's feel-good chemicals and so it  will make you happier and better able to  study and also if you're something other  than just working to look forward to  especially if you take your exercise  with a friend like going running with a  friends a great idea probably the most  important thing though is to  consistently eat a good diet and when I  say that I don't just mean eat salad I  mean eat a balanced diet with plenty of  protein fish if you can enough calories  not too many sugary things there's a  tendency amongst people to few late  night study sessions sometimes all study  sessions with sugary foods to give you a  buzz and a sugar high and yeah you're  going to get a sugar high for a bit but  the low it comes after that it's just  going to make you want to give up  studying for the night also a UCLA study  recently showed that a diet high in  sugar significantly hampers the brain's  ability to synthesize new information  and recall old information and this  isn't to say that you shouldn't eat any  sugary foods just try and cut down where  you can it's the small changes that  make a big difference like drinking diet  soft drinks instead of regular ones or  stead of having chocolate as a snack  having nuts also drink plenty of water  it's a really common problem to get  dehydrated while studying so keep a  bottle of water close to hand and keep  drinking throughout the day rule number  five work really hard I've saved this  point to last because if I'd mentioned  at any earlier it's would have put  people off but also because it is the  most important point to make none of  what I've said before is going to count  for anything  if you're not willing to put the hard  work in the only way that anyone's ever  done anything ever is by putting in lots  and lots of hard work the whole genius  being 1% inspiration 99% perspiration  quote is true even Einstein one of the  most intelligent scientists that ever  lived took ten years to form his theory  of general relativity and the theory is  a triumph it's one of those incredible  accomplishments that humankind has ever  managed but if he hadn't sat down and  thought about it for ten years put in  the hours of doing really really hard  maths we wouldn't have a theory of  relativity if you want to succeed the  only way to do it is to sit down and get  on with it to put in the hours of work  to put in the blood sweat and tears but  hard work in itself does not guarantee  success ultimately it's not about what  you put into your studying that counts  is about what you get out of your study  that counts and that's exactly where all  stuff I talked about previously comes in  if you're willing to put in those hours  of effort the dozens of hours of sitting  down and doing hard work then you need  to make sure that what you put in counts  if you're going to work hard make sure  that your learning is tailored to you  manage your time and manage your notes  and take good care of yourself if you do  all those things then the work that you  appear in is going to be worth so much  more best of luck

